    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      Expanded Metal vs Perforated Metal: Which One for Your Application
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 9 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Choose <strong>expanded metal</strong> when you want maximum strength and rigidity per kilogram, zero material waste, an integral anti-slip surface for walkways and platforms, or security grilles that resist cutting and prying — it is made by slitting and stretching a solid sheet, so the metal's grain flows continuously through every strand. Choose <strong>perforated metal</strong> when you need precise, uniform hole geometry (round, square, slotted, micro-perforation), predictable open area for filtration and screening, a smooth finished surface, or architectural appearance control. Cost-wise, expanded metal is typically 15–30% cheaper per square meter for equivalent thickness because nothing is punched out and scrapped. The decision table and application breakdowns below will place you in under two minutes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
