    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Expanded metal and perforated metal solve overlapping problems — ventilation, guarding, screening, drainage — with fundamentally different engineering. Buyers routinely specify one when the other would perform better and cost less, usually because the quote they received first defined the conversation. This guide fixes that. For product-level specifications, see <a href="/products/expanded-metal/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">expanded metal</a> and <a href="/products/perforated-metal/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">perforated metal</a>; the sections below explain when each process wins.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Process -->
    <section id="process" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Two Different Processes — and Why It Matters</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Expanded metal</strong> starts as a solid coil or sheet. A die slits a pattern of short parallel cuts, then the sheet is stretched perpendicular to the cuts. The slits open into diamond-shaped apertures bounded by raised strands; no material is removed. A 1,000 kg sheet becomes roughly 1,000 kg of expanded mesh (allowing a few percent for edge trim). The strands are continuous with the parent sheet — the original grain structure, and any coating or temper, runs unbroken through every strand and bond.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Perforated metal</strong> is made on a punch press or turret punch: holes are punched out of the sheet in a pattern, and the punched-out material — typically 10–25% of the sheet weight for common open areas — falls out as scrap. That scrap is recycled, but its cost (plus the punching energy and tool wear) is in your price. What you buy in return is geometric precision: holes of exact diameter, pitch, and pattern, from 0.5 mm micro-perforations up to 50 mm+ louvers, with open area controllable to tight tolerances.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Attribute</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Expanded Metal</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Perforated Metal</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Material removal</td>
              <td class="p-3">None — sheet is slit and stretched</td>
              <td class="p-3">10–25% of sheet punched out as scrap</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Aperture geometry</td>
              <td class="p-3">Diamond/strand pattern, set by tooling; semi-random appearance</td>
              <td class="p-3">Exact round, square, slot, hexagon, or custom shapes on any pitch</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Strand/bond continuity</td>
              <td class="p-3">Continuous with parent sheet — no welds, no punched edges</td>
              <td class="p-3">Interrupted at every hole; stress concentrates at hole edges</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Surface</td>
              <td class="p-3">Raised strands (flattened version available); naturally anti-slip</td>
              <td class="p-3">Flat, smooth; anti-slip only with special patterns or treatments</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Standard sheet size</td>
              <td class="p-3">Commonly 4 × 8 ft / 1.2 × 2.4 m; mesh-count designations per strand thickness</td>
              <td class="p-3">4 × 8 ft / 1 × 2 m standard; any size up to press bed limits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Tooling for custom work</td>
              <td class="p-3">Low — standard dies cover most diamond sizes</td>
              <td class="p-3">Punch tools cost money; new hole patterns need new tools</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The engineering takeaway: expanded metal behaves like a formed structure, perforated metal behaves like a weakened plate with engineered holes. That single difference drives every comparison below.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Strength -->
    <section id="strength" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Strength-to-Weight Comparison</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Because expanded metal keeps 100% of the sheet material in continuous strands, it delivers exceptional rigidity for its weight. A 3 mm strand raised expanded metal walkway panel, weighing roughly 12–18 kg/m² depending on mesh, will typically out-stiffen a perforated plate of similar mass — the perforated plate loses section area at every hole and its load path is interrupted thousands of times per panel. In shear and impact, expanded metal's bonded intersections distribute loads across the whole sheet; perforated plate concentrates stress at hole edges, which is where fatigue cracks start under cyclic loading.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Perforated metal's strength profile is more predictable, not weaker in absolute terms: for a given sheet thickness, engineers can calculate net-section properties precisely (gross area minus hole area, with standard stress-concentration factors), which is why perforated plate is the default for pressure-bearing applications like filter baskets, screens, and machine guards with certification requirements. If your design needs documented structural calculations, perforated usually gets there faster; if it needs the stiffest, lightest, most abuse-tolerant panel, expanded usually wins.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Practical Rule</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          For equivalent load-bearing performance, expanded metal can typically be specified one strand thickness lighter than the perforated plate it replaces — which offsets much of the perforated option's scrap-cost disadvantage in heavy-duty applications like catwalks, ramps, and truck decking.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Cost -->
    <section id="cost" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Cost Comparison</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        On raw material alone, expanded metal wins by 15–30% for comparable thickness: the perforated producer pays for the full sheet and sells you 75–90% of it, while the expander sells you effectively everything. Typical FOB China ranges for standard carbon steel product illustrate the gap:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Product (Carbon Steel, FOB China)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Range</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Cost Drivers</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Expanded metal, raised, 3 mm strand (~20 kg/m² class)</td>
              <td class="p-3">$14–24/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">Sheet cost plus expansion; minimal waste</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Perforated plate, 3 mm, round holes, 40% open area</td>
              <td class="p-3">$22–38/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">Sheet cost + scrap + punching time; holes are effectively paid for twice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Expanded metal, flattened &amp; galvanized</td>
              <td class="p-3">$18–30/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">Adds flattening pass and zinc</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Perforated stainless 304, 1.5 mm, fine perforation</td>
              <td class="p-3">$35–70/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">Stainless sheet premium plus slower punching on fine tools</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Three situations close or reverse the gap. First, very high open area (above ~60%): perforated plate becomes expensive because so much material is scrapped, but expanded metal with extra-large diamond apertures also needs heavier parent sheet to keep strand width — compare both quotes. Second, thin gauges below about 0.8 mm: expanded metal machinery handles micro-mesh poorly, while perforators run thin coil efficiently; perforated wins below this line. Third, standard perforation patterns in high volume: tool amortization disappears and punching is fast, so commodity perforated sheet in 4 × 8 ft can approach expanded pricing. Use the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">FOB price calculator</a> to compare quoted panels on a landed basis, and our <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">weight calculator</a> mindset applies here too: normalize by kg/m² before accepting any per-sheet price.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Walkways & security -->
    <section id="walkways" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Anti-Slip, Walkways, and Security Applications</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        This is expanded metal's home territory. The raised strand pattern gives an inherently slip-resistant surface in all directions — no added treatment, no wear layer to disappear — which is why raised expanded metal is the standard for industrial walkways, stair treads, work platforms, maintenance catwalks, and trailer decking. Drainage is instant (water, mud, snow fall straight through), sight lines are preserved, and a dropped tool usually lands on the deck below rather than becoming a projectile. Serrated-strand variants add grip for oily or icy environments. Perforated plate can serve the same duty only with raised-button patterns, embossed dimples, or applied grit coatings, all of which add cost and eventually wear.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        For security, the same physics applies in a different register. Expanded metal grilles and mesh for window guards, machine enclosures, and storage cages have no discrete wires to cut one at a time and no weak weld points; attacks must sever continuous metal strands. Heavy flattened expanded metal (5 mm+ strands) is used for bank screens and ballistic-facing barriers. Perforated plate also performs well in security — it is the material of armored doors and vault panels — but for cage-style security at commodity cost, expanded metal is the standard choice, and the reason <a href="/applications/fence/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">fence and barrier</a> applications overwhelmingly specify it for infill panels and anti-climb toppings.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Fire and code considerations tip further toward expanded metal in walkways: open mesh allows sprinkler penetration and smoke venting through flooring, a genuine advantage in multi-tier racking and mezzanine applications where solid decking complicates fire suppression design.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Filtration -->
    <section id="filtration" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Filtration and Screening</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Filtration is perforated metal's home territory, for one decisive reason: open area and hole size are exactly specified and uniform across the sheet. A 1 mm hole on 2 mm staggered pitch is 22.7% open area everywhere, which makes flow calculations, pressure-drop modeling, and particle cutoff behavior reproducible — the working assumptions behind most <a href="/applications/filtration/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">filtration and screening</a> designs. Perforated plate serves as support and drainage layers for sintered and woven filter media, as vibrating screen decks, as diffusers, and as protection shells around filter elements. Fine perforation down to 0.5 mm and below handles preliminary dewatering and seed/grain separation.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Expanded metal also appears in filtration — as filter leaf frames, rotary drum screens for coarse separation, and strainer baskets where structural robustness matters more than cutoff precision — but its diamond apertures are inherently less uniform, and strand geometry complicates flow modeling. For any application with a defined micron or particle-size requirement, pair perforated or woven media rather than relying on expanded metal alone. If your application spans fine mesh, see our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-wire-mesh-for-filtration/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">choosing wire mesh for filtration</a> for the woven-mesh side of the design.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Decision table -->
    <section id="decision" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Decision Table</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
