              <td class="p-3"><strong>Expanded</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">Continuous strands, no cut points, no welds to fail</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Filtration, precise open area, pressure drop specs</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>Perforated</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">Exact hole geometry, reproducible flow, certifiable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Architectural facades and interior screens</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>Perforated</strong> (often)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Pattern control, image-perforation options, smooth finish</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Lowest cost structural mesh panel</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>Expanded</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">No material waste; 15–30% cheaper per m²</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Thin gauge below 0.8 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>Perforated</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">Coil-fed punching handles thin stock efficiently</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Certified structural calculations required</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>Perforated</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">Net-section properties straightforward to document</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Ventilation + visibility + security combined</td>
              <td class="p-3"><strong>Expanded</strong></td>
              <td class="p-3">One product delivers all three at commodity cost</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">When the Answer Is Both</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Some assemblies legitimately combine the two: expanded metal outer guards over perforated baffles, or perforated support plates carrying expanded metal wear surfaces. If a supplier pushes one material for every component, that is a catalog talking, not engineering. Get both quoted — standard expanded dies and standard perforation tools both carry short lead times from cluster factories.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is expanded metal stronger than perforated metal?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Per unit weight, generally yes. Expanded metal keeps all the parent sheet's material in continuous strands with bonded intersections, giving it excellent rigidity and impact resistance for its mass. Perforated plate of the same thickness is weaker in net section but far more predictable: engineers can calculate exact net-area properties with standard stress-concentration factors. For uncertified structural panels like walkways and guards, expanded typically wins; for documented, calculable structures, perforated is easier to certify.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Which is cheaper, expanded metal or perforated metal?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Expanded metal is typically 15–30% cheaper per square meter at equivalent parent-sheet thickness, because punching removes 10–25% of the material as scrap while expanding removes none. The gap narrows or reverses for very thin gauges (under ~0.8 mm), very high open areas, or high-volume standard perforation patterns where tooling is fully amortized. Compare quotes normalized to kg/m² and run them through the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">FOB price calculator</a> before deciding.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can expanded metal be used for anti-slip walkways?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes — raised (standard) expanded metal is one of the most common anti-slip walkway materials in industry. The raised strand pattern grips in all directions, cannot wear off like applied coatings, and sheds water, oil, mud, and snow through the open mesh. Serrated-strand variants add grip for extreme conditions. Specify strand thickness for the load class (3–4.5 mm strands for pedestrian duty, heavier for vehicle ramps) and confirm support-frame spacing with the supplier's load tables.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What open area can I get with each material?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Perforated metal offers precise, uniform open area from a few percent to about 65% (higher with special patterns like straight-row slots), in round, square, slot, hexagon, and custom shapes. Expanded metal typically delivers 30–70% open area depending on diamond size and strand width, but the figure varies slightly across the sheet and is described by mesh designations rather than exact hole dimensions. For flow-critical or filtration work requiring a stated open percentage, choose perforated; for ventilation and visibility where approximate openness suffices, expanded works well.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Which is better for architectural and decorative applications?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Usually perforated metal, because hole size, pitch, and pattern are fully controllable — including image perforation for facades — and the surface stays flat and smooth for finishing (anodizing, powder coating, PVD). Expanded metal also appears architecturally for its distinctive diamond texture and transparency gradients, but pattern control is coarser. For interior screens and ceiling panels, both are common; for branded facade work, perforated is the default.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

